{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"$f(x)$ is the product of two, more basic, expressions: $\\cos(x)$ and $x^{-2}$.\n\nTherefore, the derivative of $f$ can be found using **the product rule**:\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{d}{dx}[u(x)v(x)]\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}[u(x)]v(x)+u(x)\\dfrac{d}{dx}[v(x)]\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=u'(x)v(x)+u(x)v'(x)\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}f'(x)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}(\\cos(x)x^{-2})\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}(\\cos(x))x^{-2}+\\cos(x)\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^{-2})\u0026\u0026\\gray{\\text{The product rule}}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-\\sin(x)x^{-2}+\\cos(x)(-2)x^{-3}\u0026\u0026\\gray{\\text{Differentiate }\\cos(x)\\text{ and }x^{-2}}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-\\sin(x)x^{-2}-2\\cos(x)x^{-3}\u0026\u0026\\gray{\\text{Simplify}}\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion,\n\n$f'(x)=-\\sin(x)x^{-2}-2\\cos(x)x^{-3}$\n\nor any other equivalent form.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"Let $f(x)=\\cos(x)x^{-2}$.\n\n$f'(x)=$ [[☃ expression 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"expression 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answerForms":[{"considered":"correct","form":false,"key":0,"simplify":false,"value":"-\\sin(x)x^{-2}-2\\cos(x)x^{-3}"}],"buttonSets":["basic","trig","prealgebra"],"functions":["f","g","h"],"times":false},"static":false,"type":"expression","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
